Date: Mon, 22 Feb 93 18:21:36 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: ECON: Cover's ideas

   From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)

   One paper had some results which I found amazingly paradoxical.  Suppose
   there is an investment, say a stock, which in each period will either
   double in value or lose half its value.  If these happened with 50-50
   likelihood, then an investment in this stock would have no net gain over
   a period of time.  This points out the importance of looking at "log
   return"; a factor of 2 and a factor of 1/2 are equal and opposite in
   log terms.

The trouble is that this is not true.  A stock that doubles or halves
randomly over a period of time has an average return of 25% per unit
time:

	(2 + 0.5)/2 = 1.25

(The expected value of an investment will be 1.25 times what you
bought it for.)

Over time, these returns multiply in the expected way.  For instance,
after 2 units of time:

	outcome	probability
	0.25	1/4
	1.	2/4
	4.	1/4

Giving an expected value of (0.25/4 + 1/2 + 4/4) = 1.5625 (= 1.25 * 1.25).

So you're stock market is going *up*, at a very good pace.

(Intuitively, you can see this "must" be true, because no company is
going broke.)

Dale

