Date: Mon, 22 Feb 93 16:27:52 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: ECON: Cover's ideas

Steve Whitrow points out that some light can be shed on the paradox I
described in Cover's writings by looking at it in betting terms.

Suppose someone is offering you a betting game in which for each dollar
you put in, you get back either $0.50 or $2.00, with 50-50 odds.  As
Steve says, this is a positive expected gain - if you do this many itmes,
you would end up with $1.25 on the average.

Clearly, this is a favorable offer.  What is the best way to respond to
it?  Cover shows that (at least if you are going to do it multiple times)
your best strategy is to put half your money into the bet.  After each
win or loss, you reallocate your funds to keep half your money there.

Here again it seems paradoxical.  You've got this fantastic,
no-strings-attached offer, with a considerable positive return.  And the
more money you put in on any round, the more you will make on average.
Wouldn't you think you should put as much money as you could find into
the bet?  If your friend is giving away money, you want the biggest
bucket around!

But that's not the best way to go.  If you continually put all your money
into the bet, you win half and lose half, and end up doing a random walk
up and down, not getting much of anywhere.  By keeping only half your
money there, and reallocating after each bet, you steadily make progress.
I ran some simulations and your money grows incredibly fast.

I wonder if there is some variation on this which could be presented as a
"sucker bet" to con people.  What if you did it with a 60% chance of the
$0.50 payoff, and a 40% chance of getting $2.00?  These average out to
$1.10 so it is still a positive payoff to the customer.  But if he just
puts his money in and "lets it ride", he will over time end up with less
money than he started with.

What if you offered this deal to the mark, with the proviso that he had
to put in his money and leave it there throughout, and you would run the
bet 20 times?  You tell him that each time he expects a 10% increase, and
over with 20 repetitions that means he should increase his money by over
a factor of 6x.  But actually, according to simulations I've run, he'll
end up with around a quarter for each dollar he puts in.  I wonder if
anyone would take this offer.

Hal
