Date: Mon, 22 Feb 93 17:15:39 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: ECON: Cover's ideas

Dale Worley writes:

> The trouble is that this is not true.  A stock that doubles or halves
> randomly over a period of time has an average return of 25% per unit
> time:

On considering this, I can see that Dale is right.  This removes much of
the paradoxical nature of the results I found in Cover's papers.  It also
means that the only sucker in the "sucker bet" I presented a few minutes
ago is the person who offers it: the simulations I ran were inadequate;
there is a small chance of having to make a very large payoff which makes
the whole game completely favorable to the player.

What Cover's algorithm for maximizing expected log return does is to turn
a 25%, wildly varying return into a 6%, rather stable return.  He has
taken a loss in linear return in order to maximize log return.  That does
not sound so smart.

Cover alludes to some debate about whether log return is the best measure
of investment strategy: "A discussion of the naturalness of this
objective can be found in the series of papers by Williams, Kelly,
Latane, Breiman, Thorp, Samuelson, Hakansson, Bell and Cover, and Arrow."
I wondered why, if the idea was so "natural", he had to list so many
authors to justify it.  Now I see from this discussion that there is
considerable question as to whether maximizing log return makes sense
at all.  It sounds like you're paying money out of your pocket, at
least in this example.

Thanks to Dale for helping shed light on this issue!

Hal

