Date: Mon, 22 Feb 93 17:35:33 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: EXT: Selective breeding

Here is an argument for the idea that children are a form of immortality.

As Extropians, we expect to dramatically grow and change over the
millenia.  We will expand our consciousness far beyond what we know as
life today.  Inevitably, in doing so, we will transcend our current
limited mentalities and become beings far beyond anything we can
imagine today.

Now, with such dramatic changes, how much of what I am today will govern
what I will be a thousand years from now?  Not much, obviously.  My
limited existance of today will be only a tiny fraction of the being I
expect to become.

In this sense, then, I am little more than the seed of the person I
will be in the future.  Our ordinary notions of identity and continuity
break down in the face of such dramatic changes.  All I can really hope
for is to initiate a chain of events which starts with me and leads to
greatness.

Even if I don't manage to survive into the future, though, I can still
have a comparable influence: through my actions, and through the people I
may interact with who live on beyond me.  My influence over the
developing hearts and minds of my children may be comparable to the
amount of influence the I-of-today will have over the person I become in
the far future.

Put another way, if I were to look back 1000 years from now on where I
came from, how I came to become what I am, it's possible that I would
identify many "sources" other than the particular individual I was as a
youth.  I may not even remember who I was originally, assuming the notion
even makes sense - brain merging and such may make it ambiguous.

My parents, in a sense, live on in me; and, less directly, my more
remote ancestors do as well.  All of them shaped what I have become and
in the same way I will shape those who come after me.  From this point of
view, other forms of survival may be reasonable alternatives to simply
living a long time.

Hal

