We agree that the implication (PPL --> libertarian) is false.  For  
voluntarists, the implication  (libertarian --> PPL) is true.  For  
voluntarists, libertarian PPLs thus form a proper subset of PPLs.   
Customs may vary on the fuzzy boundaries of the concepts  
"initiation," "force," "human" and so on.  These fuzzy boundaries  
give rise to a [multiple-dimensioned] spectrum of "libertarian" PPLs.   
The libertarian non-coercion principle (NCP) applies only to  
transactions between me and mine vs. you and yours. When we have no  
notion of individually owned property (e.g., self-ownership), then  
the NCP does not apply to any transaction.  It therefore holds  
trivially in such a society (the case you raise). But, is such a  
degenerate system interesting to consider?

-Pete

