   American wadget-makers complain of Florinese competition.  Uncle Sam
   announces that if you bring Florinese wadgets into the USA for sale, you may
   go to jail.

   Now, Uncle Sam never bought a single Florinese wadget in his life, so how
   can Sam make the threat Dale mentioned?

OK, here's what I meant to say, but probably didn't make clear:  Since
nations have the power to regulate international trade by their
nationals, we can model international trade as a market with about 180
participants (or whatever the number of nations is at the moment) --
each nation's nationals are coerced into acting as agents of the
"nation's will".

The interesting thing about this market is that some players in the
market have the ability to abuse others -- the usual "if you don't do
X, I'll cut off your trade in Y" sort of stuff.  Generally, the
threatening players are the industrialized countries (Benin can't make
credible threats in the international markets).  And often the threats
are directed toward countries against whom the threatener would not
use military force -- the situation isn't "coercive" in the strict
definition of the word, but it is nasty.

I think this might be a useful example to study to see how players
in a market can use their economic power to apply pressure to other
players.  (And how such attempts fail.)

   Elementary economics dictates that the latter threat, on the other
   hand, is harmful: whatever benefit it gives American wadget-makers
   is more than balanced by harm to American wadget-buyers.

It all depends on your utility function.  In the elementary economic
sense, restraining trade is always harmful, but that measures benefit
solely by total economic activity.  If the utility function of a
nation (or of the people in it) contains factors involving the
"equity" of distribution of economic activity, restraining trade can
be "beneficial".  It all depends on what you count as "good".

Dale

